  "slug": "edge-non-hermitian-4a89",
  "summary": "Recent preprints report edge amplification, self-dual spectral singularities and symmetry-breaking constraints in non-Hermitian gain-loss systems.",
  "synthesis": "The four preprints share a common setting: open non-Hermitian systems with gain, loss, or asymmetric coupling, where scattering and spectral properties depart from Hermitian expectations. One item reports an experimental microwave study of universal statistics of reflectivity, transmissivity and absorptivity in multi-port non-Hermitian scattering systems. Another shows that coupled skin-effect channels amplify states bouncing off edges, departing from the usual OBC-within-PBC spectral picture. A third is a theoretical and numerical study of a self-dual spectral singularity in coupled gain and loss ring resonators coupled to a waveguide. The fourth shows that gain-loss induced T-symmetry breaking is forbidden in time-dependent systems, even for random gain-loss distributions. What is new is the combination of statistical universality, edge amplification, and constraints on symmetry breaking, rather than a single unified result. Edge-state physics is represented by only one of the four items.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
